<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      Overrides the .NET MAUI Android SDK's own default Maui.MainTheme (bundled inside the MAUI
      workload, not a file in this project) to fix a real crash hit on a physical device (Samsung
      Galaxy S23+, dark mode) the first time this app's Android head was ever actually launched —
      every cold start crashed with:

        java.lang.IllegalArgumentException: This component requires that you specify a valid
        TextAppearance attribute. Update your app theme to inherit from Theme.MaterialComponents.
          at com.google.android.material.navigation.NavigationBarView.<init>
          at com.google.android.material.bottomnavigation.BottomNavigationView.<init>
          at com.microsoft.maui.PlatformInterop.createNavigationBar
          at ShellItemRenderer.onCreateView

      Root cause: the SDK default Maui.MainTheme points bottomNavigationViewStyle at the OLD
      Android Design-support-library style (@style/Widget.Design.BottomNavigationView), which
      doesn't declare the itemTextAppearanceActive/Inactive attributes the actual widget being
      created — com.google.android.material.bottomnavigation.BottomNavigationView, used to render
      AppShell.xaml's TabBar on Android — requires. Fixed by pointing it at the real Material
      Components equivalent instead. Same resource name ("Maui.MainTheme") as the SDK's own
      definition, so Android's resource merger lets this project-level override win — standard,
      supported way to customize it, not a hack.
    -->
    <style name="Maui.MainTheme" parent="Maui.MainTheme.Base">
        <item name="bottomNavigationViewStyle">@style/Widget.MaterialComponents.BottomNavigationView</item>
    </style>
</resources>
